1. Objective & Technical Scope
The purpose of this module was to implement non-destructive slicing operations on Series datasets. Slicing allows engineering applications to extract specific contiguous windows of data points without mutating or destroying the original dataset.

2. My Implementation Process
Here is how I designed and validated the slicing functionality:

Defining the Base Dataset:
I instantiated the baseline Series container scores holding five floating-point values representing raw assessment results ({55.0, 80.5, 92.0, 43.5, 67.0}).

Executing the Slicing Operation:
I called scores.slice(1, 4); to extract elements from start index 1 (inclusive) up to end index 4 (exclusive).
This sub-range isolated three specific entries (indices 1, 2, and 3: 80.5, 92.0, and 43.5).

Ensuring Non-Destructive Memory Management:
I designed the .slice() method to allocate and return a completely new Series instance (sliced_scores) rather than editing the existing buffer in-place.
The original scores series retained all 5 original elements, ensuring side-effect-free data pipelines.

Slicing Verification:
I queried sliced_scores.size() to confirm that the generated subset container contained exactly 3 elements.
3. Verification & Results
The execution of series_slicing.cpp verified that sliced_scores.size() returned 3. The output confirmed that contiguous sub-series can be cleanly sliced, copied, and passed down analytical workflows without altering original raw source data.
